NAME
    ed - the line editor

SYNOPSIS
    ed [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
    A POSIX-style line editor. Reads commands on stdin, writes
    diagnostic output (just "?" on error) to stdout. Loads FILE
    into the in-memory buffer at start; w FILE saves the buffer
    back to disk via sys_fs_write.

    For interactive use, vi is friendlier. ed is here because (a)
    it's small and reliable, (b) it works over a serial line
    where vi's cursor positioning doesn't, and (c) it's the
    canonical Unix line editor.

COMMANDS
    .                   end-of-input marker (in input mode)
    N                   set current line to N and print it
    .,$p                print from current line to end
    1,$p                print whole buffer
    a                   append text after current line (end with .)
    i                   insert text before current line (end with .)
    c                   change current line (end with .)
    d                   delete current line
    s/pat/repl/         substitute first match on current line
    s/pat/repl/g        substitute all matches
    w [FILE]            write buffer to FILE (default: original)
    q                   quit (refuses if unsaved changes)
    Q                   quit unconditionally
    =                   print current line number
    /pat/               search forward for pat

EXAMPLES
    ed hello.txt
    a
    line 1
    line 2
    .
    w
    q

EXIT STATUS
    0   normal quit
    1   command syntax error or write failure

SEE ALSO
    vi(1), cat(1)

NOTES
    Pattern syntax is literal substring, NOT regex (matching grep).
